# Programmatic command reports

Use <Symbol path="cargo_upwell::run_command" /> when an integration wants the same presentation-neutral `check` or `doctor` policy as the CLI rather than the lower-level complete probe object. The command module is private; the supported command functions and report types are re-exported from the `cargo_upwell` crate root.

## Run a command synchronously

<Example title="Produce a check report">

```rust
use cargo_upwell::{CancellationToken, CommandKind, run_command};
use semver::VersionReq;

let cancellation = CancellationToken::new();
let report = run_command(
    CommandKind::Check,
    &request,
    &cancellation,
);

let supported = VersionReq::parse("^0.20")?;
if !report.schema_matches(&supported) {
    return Err(format!("unsupported report schema {}", report.schema).into());
}

let json = report.to_json()?;
std::fs::write("target/upwell/check.json", json)?;
```

Like `run_probe`, `run_command` is synchronous and takes request/token references. `run_command_with_options` adds `ProbeOptions` for Cargo presentation. The function returns a <Symbol path="cargo_upwell::CommandReport" /> even for command failure so callers can retain structured evidence.

</Example>

## Read the report contract

The report includes:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `schema` | semantic compatibility version for this command report |
| `command` | `Check` or `Doctor` |
| `outcome` | stable presentation-neutral result category |
| `target` | selected package/binary facts when discovery completed |
| `application` | generated document identity when the probe responded |
| `framework_version` | version reported by the target when available |
| `protocol` | selected protocol when preparation completed |
| `diagnostics` | canonical structured diagnostics |
| `checks` | ordered Doctor health checks |
| `exit_code()` | stable process exit category for CLI-compatible policy |

Call `schema_matches` before depending on fields from another tool version. `to_json()` is deterministic machine-readable report serialization; terminal rendering is not the contract.

## Separate reports from probe envelopes

Use a command report for an operational `check`/`doctor` decision and its exit category. Use a <Symbol path="upwell_tooling_schema::ProbeEnvelope" /> when an artifact collector needs the target's canonical success/failure process envelope. Use a `ToolingDocument` only when preparation succeeded and downstream graph/document work requires the exact application model.

Do not flatten these into one ad hoc JSON type. They have separate schema versions and completeness rules.

## Apply an error policy

The validated CLI policy maps success, validation, command misuse, target selection, Cargo build, probe, and operational failures into stable exit categories. Programmatic consumers should call `report.exit_code()` rather than reproduce numeric mappings from prose.

Preserve diagnostics before branching. A non-success report can still identify the target, include framework/protocol facts, and explain the failure. `Doctor` checks remain ordered execution evidence rather than an unordered map.

<Callout type="warning" title="A report return is not command success">

`run_command` returning a `CommandReport` means command execution produced a report. Check `outcome` or `exit_code()` before continuing a deployment.

</Callout>

## Cancellation

Pass a caller-owned token and cancel it from another owner when the editor request, CI job, or service operation is abandoned:

```rust plain
use std::sync::mpsc;

let cancellation = CancellationToken::default();
let worker_token = cancellation.clone();
let (sender, receiver) = mpsc::sync_channel(1);

std::thread::spawn(move || {
    let report = run_command(CommandKind::Doctor, &request, &worker_token);
    let _ = sender.send(report);
});

if editor_request_was_cancelled() {
    cancellation.cancel();
}

let report = receiver.recv()?;
```

Cancellation is active and cooperative: the process layer kills the running Cargo metadata/build/probe process group and retains bounded output where its typed error permits it. Cancellation while waiting for the invocation lock is reported separately from a build/probe failure. `run_command` converts cancellation into an operational-failure report; lower-level `run_probe` exposes typed cancellation evidence.

## Troubleshoot reports

- CI treats every returned report as success: branch on `exit_code()`.
- A consumer expects a document on validation failure: retain an envelope/report instead.
- Numeric exit assumptions drift: call the API rather than hardcoding the table.
- Missing target facts mean discovery did not complete, not that serialization dropped fields.
- Report parsing from another version must check semantic schema compatibility first.

See <GuideRef slug="cargo-upwell/programmatic-api/discovery-and-probes">programmatic probes</GuideRef> and <GuideRef slug="cargo-upwell/cli/automation-and-completions">automation and completions</GuideRef>.
